### ============================================================
###  Jobs API — /api/jobs/**
###  The async backbone: submit work, read its state, stream it.
###  Run auth.http first to populate {{access_token}}.
### ============================================================

### Submit an operator invocation as a job.
### Same body as /invoke; answers 202 with the job id. Copy it into {{jobId}} below.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/operators/schema-integration/submit
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "operation": "ping",
  "parameters": {}
}

### Read one job. Reaches SUCCEEDED with the ping payload in `result`.
### Another tenant's job id answers 404 — indistinguishable from one that does not exist.
@jobId = replace-with-the-jobId-from-the-submit-above
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/jobs/{{jobId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Accept: application/json

### List recent jobs for this tenant, newest first (limit defaults to 20, caps at 100).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/jobs?limit=20
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Accept: application/json

### List recent jobs for one project.
# GET {{baseUrl}}/api/jobs?projectId={{projectId}}&limit=20
# Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}

### Stream a job's state changes (SSE).
###
### EventSource cannot set an Authorization header, so this one route also accepts
### the token as ?access_token=. Most .http clients do not render an event stream —
### use curl for this one, against a *freshly submitted* job so there is something
### left to watch:
###
###   curl -N "http://localhost:8081/api/jobs/$JOB_ID/events?access_token=$TOKEN"
###
### It emits the current state immediately, one `job` event per change, and closes
### the stream once the job is terminal.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/jobs/{{jobId}}/events
Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
Accept: text/event-stream
